      title: 'Grandma Celestine’s word list',
      body: 'Jalen was sixteen and bored when Pastor Mbemba asked the youth group at Greater Mount Olive to help with the new members’ potluck. Half the new families were from the Congo, and the sanctuary on Sunday now carried French, Lingala and Kikongo along with the Houston drawl. Jalen’s job was to label the dishes. Mama Celestine, who was eighty if she was a day, kept correcting his spelling. "Not madesu like that," she laughed. "Ma-de-su. Beans. You write it the way it sounds." By the third dish he had a notebook out. By the fifth, she was teaching him. Mbote, hello, and also good. Bu, she said, was a little front piece that could turn a word into a thing you could hold in your mind, like goodness. Jalen had just learned, in a Bible class of all places, about a scholar from Brazzaville named Obenga who argued that ancient Egyptian built words like goodness the same way. He showed Mama Celestine the picture on his phone. She put on her glasses. "Brazzaville," she said slowly. "That is my city." For an hour the potluck forgot to eat. Grandparents who had not spoken in English all morning were trading words across the table: Lingala, Kikongo, Wolof from the Senegalese couple, a Gullah saying from Deacon Pinckney, who was born on St. Helena Island and had been told as a boy to stop talking that way. Jalen wrote every word down with the name of the person who said it. Pastor Mbemba finally clapped his hands for the blessing. Before he prayed, he held up Jalen’s notebook. "Somebody wanted us to forget how we talk," he said. Then he read them the proverb he had preached on that morning: "The lip of truth shall be established for ever: but a lying tongue is but for a moment." (Proverbs 12:19) He handed the notebook back. "Write it down, young man. Keep writing." Jalen kept the notebook. It is in his backpack now, half full, with a column on the right where he marks which words he has checked and which ones still need an elder to confirm.',
