      body: 'Tasha’s cousin Marcus had found the proof, he said, and he held up his phone over the potato salad so the whole family reunion could see it. A post with a hundred thousand likes claimed that one word in an African language sounded just like a word in Japanese, so the two peoples must be family. "It’s right there," Marcus said. "Same word." Aunt Lorraine, who taught high school Spanish for thirty-one years, wiped her hands on a napkin. "Baby, let me ask you something. How do you say no in Spanish?" "No," said Marcus. "And in English?" "No." "So are English and Spanish the same language?" Marcus opened his mouth and closed it. The uncles laughed. Tasha had just done a Bible study on Théophile Obenga, the Congolese scholar who told a room full of professors in 1974 that one look-alike word proves nothing. She pulled up her notes. "He said you need sound-laws," she explained. "Patterns that repeat by rule. Like, in old Egyptian you put bw in front of good to make goodness. In Kongo, mbote is good and bu-bote is goodness, and mbi is bad and bu-bi is badness. It keeps happening. That is a pattern, not a coincidence." "So he proved it?" Marcus asked. "He argued it really carefully," Tasha said. "A lot of experts still disagree with his big conclusion. But his rule is how you are supposed to check." Grandpa Earl, who rarely said much, tapped his Bible on the picnic table. "The Book had that rule first," he said, and turned to Deuteronomy and read it slowly: "at the mouth of two witnesses, or at the mouth of three witnesses, shall the matter be established" (Deuteronomy 19:15). "One word is one witness, son. Bring me three." Marcus grinned and put his phone away. By the end of the afternoon he and Tasha had a list of twelve words their great-grandmother used to say, and a plan to find out where every one of them came from.',
