      body: 'Emmanuel had worked hard to lose his accent. His parents had come from Pointe-Noire when he was nine, and by fifteen he had learned that the boys at school laughed less if he sounded like them. By twenty-five, in his first job at a bank in Atlanta, nobody would have guessed he had ever spoken Lingala. One Friday his manager asked, half joking, where his family was really from, and Emmanuel heard himself say Ohio. He did not know why he said it. That evening his mother called, and her voice, warm and thick with home, filled the car as he sat in the parking deck. He answered her in clipped English. She was quiet a moment, then said in Lingala that she was praying for him. On Sunday his pastor, a Haitian brother who preached in three languages, read the account of Peter in the high priest’s courtyard. When he reached the bystanders’ words, "And after a while came unto him they that stood by, and said to Peter, Surely thou also art one of them; for thy speech bewrayeth thee." (Matthew 26:73), he stopped and looked up. "Peter could change his story," the pastor said. "He could not change his speech. And the Lord did not ask him to. He asked him to stop denying." Emmanuel sat very still. He had read that morning, for a class at church, about a scholar from the Congo named Obenga who spent his life showing that African speech is a record worth studying, not something to be ashamed of. That night he called his mother back and spoke to her in Lingala for the first time in years, stumbling, laughing at his own mistakes. On Monday he told his manager the truth: Pointe-Noire, then Atlanta. His manager just nodded and asked how to say good morning. Emmanuel told him. It was a small thing. It felt like coming home from a long way off, like Peter going out and weeping, and then being restored by the fire on the shore.',
