  title: 'Dr. Théophile Obenga — the Negro-Egyptian family and the wisdom of Egypt: his later work',
  voice: VOICE,
  bigIdea:
    'After Cairo, Obenga spent decades turning the method the symposium had found constructive into a full proposal: that Africa’s languages fall into three families, and that ancient Egyptian, Coptic, Chadic, Cushitic, Niger-Kordofanian and Nilo-Saharan form one of them, which he called Negro-Egyptian. Alongside it he wrote on African philosophy beginning with Egypt, and on Egyptian geometry and Egypt’s place in Greek learning. His message across that work is that Africa’s oldest civilization must be read as African, through its own language and thought, and that Africans must build the scholarship to do it.',
