      body: 'Dr. Malik Owens had built his reputation teaching African history at a state university in Ohio, and he had built it the hard way: footnotes, archives, two languages learned in his forties. He loved the work of men like Obenga, who had forced the academy to take African thought seriously. For years, though, his love had quietly hardened into something else. Egypt had become the answer to every question, and somewhere along the line he had stopped going to church. His mother, Mother Hattie Owens, was eighty-eight and failing. On one of his visits to her bedside she asked him to read to her, and when he reached for the history book on her nightstand she shook her head and pointed at her Bible. He opened to where her ribbon was, in First Kings, and read without thinking, and stopped at the line about Solomon’s wisdom excelling all the wisdom of Egypt: "And Solomon’s wisdom excelled the wisdom of all the children of the east country, and all the wisdom of Egypt." (1 Kings 4:30) "You see, baby," she whispered. "The Book knew Egypt was great. It just knew Who was greater." He sat a long while with the Bible open on his knees. He thought about Obenga’s careful rule, that you test every claim and do not inflate it, and realised he had stopped applying that rule to his own heart. He had let a true pride in his ancestors’ minds become a substitute for the One who made those minds. Mother Hattie went home to glory three weeks later. At the funeral, the professor stood to speak. He did not give a lecture. He told the church that his mother had taught him two things: to be proud of where we come from, and to remember Who we come from. Then he read Colossians, the verse she had underlined twice, "In whom are hid all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge." (Colossians 2:3), and said he was coming back to church, and that he meant it.',
